ical commitments to  African Americans at the same time as  you move into the twentieth century  particularly in the 1930s as you get  toward the in the response to the Great  Depression you begin to see as a result  of the great migration of  african-americans from the south to  northern cities where they were  beginning to make a difference and who  got elected because of the sheer numbers  Democrats began to reach out to African  Americans so you had a push on one hand  and a pull on the other and I think that  push and pull has helped to set up what  we have right now which is a  circumstance in which ninety percent of  African Americans vote for Democrats now  that is an enormous number but it was  you know if they were polling them when  you look at all the historical data is  quite clear that even more African  Americans supported the Republican Party  during the abolition movement and during  reconstruction so how did we get from a  circumstance in which the Republicans  had near unanimous support from African  Americans to now near unanimous  opposition so that's that's some of the  focus that I try to build from and so  this push and pull has set off a  multi-generational movement in which  African  Americans have been at some points  marginalized and demonized in Republican  politics there is a movement that took  place in the post Reconstruction era  through the early 20th century called  the lily-white movement in which the  Republican Party literally began to  purge African Americans from positions  of authority in many southern states but  most notably in Texas and Louisiana and  again this is not the sort of message  you want to send to people that you  believe ought to be a part of what you  are all about and so what we have now is  a situation as you fast-forward to our  contemporary period the last generation  or so you know it's very interesting  Richard Nixon has gone down in history  as you know the perpetrator of some some  great fraud against